    "enParagraphs": [
      "The subject of existence, typically examined within the domain of philosophy, is perhaps not yet widely addressed in scientific circles due to shortcomings in contemporary scientific methodology. Even if the question of how the primeval genesis transpired remains within the bounds of science, asking it and embarking upon research on this topic will, in all likelihood, orient you toward philosophy. Originating from the conviction that science ought to have a voice on such profound matters, this work developed as a series of independent inquiries into concepts deemed vital for investigating existence. Through cross-references between chapters, the interrelationships among these concepts have also been emphasized. Inquiries spanning biology, physics, mathematics, systems science, computer science, and statistics inevitably had to touch upon philosophy in certain domains—a circumstance that should be interpreted not as science and philosophy opposing one another, but as mutually reinforcing. Although the discussions falling within physics are more detailed compared to other fields, this is not because physics is inherently more crucial to the question of existence, but because the author's formal education was largely situated within physics. Despite the laws of physics holding true across the other examined fields and providing the bedrock for their processes, the methods of physics prove insufficient because the investigated structures and concepts generally exhibit high complexity. When considered in terms of the character of the investigation, the methodologies of systems science emphasized throughout the book are regarded as more fruitful than those of physics.",
      "As will be observed when discussing natural selection—considered paramount for comprehending existence—although certain attributes of physical laws are believed necessary for selection to operate, we do not know to what extent the fine details of these laws dictate higher-level processes such as natural selection. In investigating highly complex structures such as the brain, rather than foundational physical principles or recent breakthroughs in neurology, recourse was taken to a variety of thought experiments and logical deductions. This defining characteristic throughout the book results in it being not an exhaustive scientific treatise, but rather a compendium highlighting concepts deemed essential and the open questions surrounding them. In certain subjects, attempts have been made to answer the questions posed, and certain problems solved long ago have been presented alongside their solutions. Because the chapters of the book are arranged chronologically according to the dates of their composition, earlier chapters are generally referenced, although prospective references forward also exist. Skipping a chapter in its entirety or in part should a particular topic fall outside the reader's interest will pose no obstacle to a holistic understanding."
    ],
